A||||7||88formula||88-suspension removal, need info from the one's who have done it.||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||05-26-2001||12:00 AM||||How hard/easy is it to remove whole front suspension including the sub-frame? Is the front suspension assembly heavy?<P>Thanks.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Black88GT||05-26-2001||01:10 AM||||Might want to ask Mike V. I am sure he has done in once or twice.<P>Only thing I know is I hear that everyone breaks the upper control arm shaft when removing it, so be careful.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||05-26-2001||01:25 AM||||I have done this several times.....all that holds it up are 8 bolts.  <P>Jack up the front of the car and support with jackstands.  Support the crossmember with two floor jacks (one on either side).  Remove the two bolts facing the front of the car (one on each side), then remove the two rear bolts.  The rear ones are tricky and you will likely need to use an open end wrench on the nut (it's in an awkward position, but you'll figure it out when you look at it).<P>Now you can remove the other 4 bolts.....there are 2 on each side under the upper control arm perch.  Once all of the bolts are removed you just lower it down......don't forget to remove the linkage from the steering rack to the column.  Also, make sure all brake lines and anything else attached to the crossmember are removed before you lower it down.<P>That's about it.  It really isn't hard to do and can be accomplished in an hour or so.<P>Good luck!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||05-26-2001||03:19 AM||||Mike did leave out one very important step that goes for front and rear alike...<P>After you get them removed you have to carefully crate them up and ship them to me in Nashville. Then at some point you will get my work out 88 4 cylinder coupe suspension to put back in your shiny little Formula! heheh<P>Not gonna fall for it, are you? Dang it!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||12
Z||000004||88formula||05-26-2001||10:38 AM||||Thanks MR fiero, Songman not a chance.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||05-26-2001||12:07 PM||||Dang!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon12.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||05-26-2001||05:46 PM||||Disconnecting the steering column is much easier if you take out front compartment bucket.<P>You will have to drain the coolant and loosen or remove the coolant tubes as they run under the suspension.<BR>Once the tubes are removed, a single jack in the center where the tubes can be used to drop the suspension.<P>The '88 subframe and suspension assembly is about twice as heavy as the crossmember and suspension assembly from an early car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||05-26-2001||11:52 PM||||It was a synch. I had it out in an hour. It's not that heavy either. I was able to remove it and carry the whole thing into my cellar.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
